title: Indonesian-Dutch Conference over the Fate of West Irian
subtitle: The long-awaited conference has begun!
new-page: true
tags: event
max-visits: 1
view-if: year = 1950 and month = 12

= Legacy of The Hague

<div><img src="img/round_table_conference_closing.jpg" alt="RTC" style="width: 75%;display: block;margin-top: 5px;margin: auto;margin-bottom: 5px;border: 5px solid var(--card-border-color);"></div>

In 1949, the Round Table Conference in the Hague concluded with The Netherlands granting sovereignty over the rest of the Dutch East Indies to the United States of Indonesia with the Republic as a constituent, ending the armed conflict in the archipelago. Furthermore, the agreement established the Netherlands-Indonesia Union, a loose, symbolic union connecting Indonesia and the Netherlands under the Dutch Crown. The Dutch delegation also agreed to withdraw Dutch troops "within the shortest possible time" and in turn the Indonesian delegation agreed to take over trade agreements negotiated by the Dutch East Indies and to ease economic relations between the two states. 

However disagreements came over two major issues: the debts of the Dutch colonial administration and the status of Western Irian. In particular our delegations were rightfully offended at having to cover what we saw as the costs of Dutch military action against us and insists that a sovereign Indonesia should comprise the entirety of the former Dutch East Indies. Eventually, through the intervention of the <span style="color: #B31942;">United</span> <span style="color: #0A3161;">States</span> member of the <span style="color: #009EDB;">UN</span> Commission on Indonesia, we agreed to pay part of the former colonial government's debt.

Therefore one topic remains: the fate of Western Irian. On one hand, the Indonesian delegation emphasizes the inclusion of the entire territory of the Dutch East Indies into Indonesia. On the other hand, the Dutch argues that Western Irian had no ethnic ties with the rest of the archipelago. The issue almost resulted in the talks becoming deadlocked, with either sides refusing to back down. 

Finally, on 1 November 1949, a compromise was reached: the status of Western Irian would be determined through negotiations between the United States of Indonesia and the Netherlands within a year of the transfer of sovereignty. Both sides had quarrels over the agreement, but eventually the Dutch Parliament and the <span class="tooltip-wrap">KNIP<span class="tooltip-tip"><img src="img/KNIP1.jpg" style="width: 100%;">Established on 29 August 1945, the <span style="text-decoration: underline;">Central Indonesian National Comittee</span> was an advisory body appointed to assist the president. It was later granted legislative powers and acted as the precursor to the House of Representatives.</span></span> ratified it on 21 and 14 December, respectively. Sovereignty was transferred to the United States of Indonesia on 27 December.

Now the one-year clause is nearing its end and the issue of Western Irian remains in the air.

- @irian_conference_begin: The Indonesian delegation arrives in the Netherlands..

@irian_conference_begin
new-page: true

= The Hague Special Conference

And thus to finally answer the Western Irian Question, both states agreed to meet in a special conference held in The Hague. Vice-president Hatta has stated that Indonesia could succeed in its claim only if it is prepared to give concessions, though he remains hopeful in the result of the conference. On the other hand, many, including our members, predict this meeting would not produce any meaningful progress to resolve the West Irian dispute as either sides continue to exhibit fundamental differences in their stances. In particular the Dutch have displayed a conservative attitude regarding West Irian, reluctant to let go of the last remaining piece of their supposed "crown jewel". Nevertheless, on 4 December, the long-awaited conference has begun.

In the initial stages of the conference, the delegation of Indonesia, headed by Foreign Minister Mohammad Roem, proposed a plan whereby Indonesian sovereignty over West Irian would be recognized by 27 December, but *de facto* transferred "in the middle of 1951" after a further conference regarding Dutch interest in the territory. However this proposal was quickly rejected.

Later, the Indonesian delegation offered further concessions listed in an Oral Note. The note stated that in the event of the transfer of sovereignty over Irian, the Indonesia government would, among other things, recognize and give preferential treatment to Dutch economic interests in the area, permit immigration and employment of Dutch nationals in the administration, and establish a democratic government in West Irian with the population posessing full autonomy and representation within the government.

- @dutch_response: And their response?
- @read_note: What are the actual concessions listed in the note?

@read_note

> *(1) Recognition of existing Dutch economic and financial rights and concessions plus special consideration in connection with new investments and concessions in the development and exploitation of soil and forest resources; preferential treatment for Dutch interests in such areas as trade, shipping, and industry;* <br>
> *(2) Dutchmen to be eligible for administrative employment; Pensions for Dutch official to guaranteed by the Indonesian Govemment, as in the case of Round Table Conference Agreement;* <br>
> *(3) Immigration of Dutch nationals to Irian to be permitted and due attention paid to supplying the manpower needs of West Irian;* <br>
> *(4) Incorporation of West Irian into Indonesian communication system but with due attention to the concessions granted earlier to Dutch or mixed enterprises;* <br>
> *(5) Guarantees for freedom of religion and assistance to the humanitarian work of religious mission by the Indonesian Government;* <br>
> *(6) Efforts to be made to operate a fully democratic govemment in West Irian with a representative body to established as soon as possible with the population possessing full autonomy and a voice in the government.*

- @dutch_response: And their response?

@dutch_response

The Dutch, ever so stubborn, has staunchly refused to change their position. The Dutch delegation, led by Johan Van Maarseven as Minister of Overseas Territories, has submitted a counter-proposal. The proposal suggests that the sovereignty of West Irian would be transferred to the Netherlands-Indonesia Union, with the Netherlands retaining administrative control. The proposition had been previously rejected by the Indonesian delegation when it was discussed in an <span class="tooltip-wrap">earlier conference this year<span class="tooltip-tip"><img src="img/conference_march_1950.jpg" style="width: 100%%;">As agreed, a year after the signing of the Hague Agreement, on March 24, 1950, the <span style="text-decoration: underline;">Ministerial Conference of the Indonesian-Dutch Union</span> was held in Jakarta. The meeting was intended to discuss the results of the Round Table Conference, particularly the issue of West Irian. However, the meeting went south as no compromises is able to be reached.</span></span>. Although in this instance, the Dutch added that further negotiations regarding West Irian "should be continued under the auspices of the still extant United Nations Commission for Indonesia". The Netherlands through this proposal has shown aversion in losing control of West Irian. If the proposal is to be implemented, the Netherlands through political power over the Netherlands-Indonesian Union would remain *de facto* in control and West Irian may remain in the *status quo* for an indefinite period of time.

- @indo_response: Why would we agree to this?
- @read_dproposal: What does the proposal actually states?

@read_dproposal

> *(1)  The sovereignty of Papua should be transferred to the Netherlands-Indonesian Union, with the stipulation that the de facto control over that territory and administration would remain in the Netherlands hands;* <br>
> *(2)  The negotiations should be continued under the auspices of the still extant United Nations Commission for Indo-nesia or any other organ could render any service to the make that possible negotiation.* <br>
> *(3)  since the future of the area had not been decided by negotiation within the year's period stipulated in article 2 of the Charter of the Transfer of Sovereignty, the Netherlands sovereignty and the status should be maintained.* 

- @indo_response: Why would we agree to this?

@indo_response

The Indonesian delegation, in accordance to national dignity and common sense, has firmly rejected the proposal. 

The days and weeks came and by, and soon thereafter the deadline on 27 December passed. With no progress achieved and no compromises reached, the negotiations stalled. Ultimately the conference broke up in failure and complete deadlock.

- @root: As expected, but a shame nonetheless.